# Untitled

Metal-Triazolate-Framework-Derived \(\mathbf{FeN_{4}Cl_{1}}\) Single-Atom Catalysts with Hierarchical Porosity for the Oxygen Reduction Reaction
Developing non-precious metal-based electrocatalysts with high activity towards oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) is vital to metal-air batteries and fuel cells.[1] Single-atom catalysts (SACs) with tunable electronic structures and maximum atom-utilization efficiency are coming under the spotlight.[2] Enhancing the density of single-atom sites and optimizing the electronic structure of metal centers through heteroatoms doping (such as S, O, B, P, Cl, etc.) are two promising ways to construct high-performance SACs.[3] In addition, it is also beneficial to promote fast mass transfer at the three-phase interface by optimizing the porosity of catalysts.[4] Therefore, a synthetic approach that can introduce
coordinated heteroatoms and ensure high metal atom loading density as well as provide hierarchical porosity at the same time would be highly desirable.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), a class of porous crystalline solids,[5] demonstrate excellent potential as precursors/self-sacrificial templates to prepare SACs via the pyrolysis.[6] Beyond the most commonly used zeolitic-imidazolate frameworks (ZJFs), metal-triazolate (MET) frameworks, another subclass of N-rich MOFs with unique advantages have attracted our attention. MET is constructed by octahedral coordination of divalent metal ions with N atoms in 1H-1,2,3-triazole.[7] First, the extremely high theoretical nitrogen content of \(41.7\,\mathrm{wt\,\%}\) in MET makes it a promising precursor/template for preparing high-density SACs. Second, nitrogen-doped carbon matrix with abundant mesopores can be readily obtained through a one-step pyrolysis of MET without using any templates or tedious treatments since the energetic triazole ligands can decompose violently while releasing a large amount of gas.[8]
Based on this, a \(Z\mathsf{n}/\mathsf{F e}\) bimetallic mixed-ligand MET was designed and synthesized as the precursor to obtain SACs. The ratio of \(Z\mathbf{n}\) and Fe was tuned to optimize the content of Fe single atoms in the derived SACs. A Cl-containing secondary ligand, 4,5-dichloroimidazole (dcIm), was introduced in MET to provide homogeneously dispersed Cl atoms. The lower electronegativity of Cl is expected to enhance the d-band delocalization and modify the electronic structures of Fe single atoms in the derived SACs.[9] The as-obtained catalyst \((\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC})\) possesses a \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}\) coordination configuration with a high Fe loading of \(2.78\,\mathrm{wt\,\%}\) and abundant mesopores with proportion of 0.92 (pore-volume ratio). It exhibits outstanding ORR activity with high halfwave potential \((E_{1/2})\) of \(0.91\;\mathrm{V}\) in \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH and \(E_{1/2}\) of \(0.79\,\mathrm{V}\) in \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{HClO_{4}}\) , which outperforms most of the reported MOF-derived SACs.[10] Moreover, as proof-of-concept, it presents excellent performance in zinc-air (Zn-air) battery with a maximum power density of \(170\,\mathrm{mW}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) and a specific capacity of \(801\,\mathrm{mAh\,g_{2n}}^{-1}\) , showing potentials in energy storage and conversion devices.
The preparation process of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) is illustrated in Figure 1. MET(Zn)-dcIm nanocrystals constructed by connecting \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) and 1H-1,2,3-triazole and 4,5-dichloroimidazole (dcIm) were first synthesized via a one-pot solvothermal process (Supporting Information, Figure S1). Since the M-N-M angle formed by the coordination of \(_\mathrm{N}\) at the 1, 3 positions of 1H-1,2,3-triazole with the metal ion is same as the M-N-M angle formed by dcIm and the metal ion, dcIm could be introduced as a mixed ligand in MET without changing the original topology (Figure 1a). The introduction of dcIm on
the MET skeleton could achieve uniform doping of Cl atoms. The existence of dcIm in MET(Zn)-dcIm was proved by carbon-13 nuclear magnetic resonance \(\mathrm{^{13}C}\) NMR) spectroscopy, thermogravimetric (TG) analysis, \(\Nu_{2}\) sorption measurements and energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS; Figures S2–S7). The content of Cl in MET(Zn)-dcIm is assessed as \(1.47\,\mathrm{at}\,\%\) by \(\Chi\) -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). Then, partial \(Z\mathfrak{n}^{2+}\) ions in MET(Zn)-dcIm were replaced by \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) ions via post-synthetic ion-exchange process. The isostructure of MET(Fe) and MET(Zn) assures the successful coordination of \(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}\) with six \(_\mathrm{N}\) atoms which is helpful to stabilize Fe atoms during the subsequent pyrolysis (Figure 1b; Supporting Information, Figures S8–S11, Table S1). The Fe content in Fe-doped MET(Zn)-dcIm and Fe-doped MET(Zn) are \(0.37\pm0.03\:\mathrm{wt}\,\%\) and \(0.37\pm\) \(0.02\:\mathrm{wt}\,\%\) , respectively (Table S2). Finally, \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) was obtained through pyrolyzing Fe-doped MET(Zn)-dcIm in Ar atmosphere under optimized condition (at \(900\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) with a heating rate of \(5\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\,\mathrm{min}^{-1}\) , Figure S12), followed by another heat-treatment in \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) flow at \(700\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) . MET(Zn)-dcIm and Fe-doped MET(Zn) were also treated to obtain derivatives as control samples, named pyrolyzed MET(Zn)-dcIm and \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\) , respectively.
According to the PXRD results, both \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) and \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\) were absent of metallic Fe or Fe-containing phases (Figure S13), indicating no obvious metal agglomeration
during the pyrolysis. A lower ratio of D and G peak areas \(\mathrm{(Area_{p}/A r e a_{c})}\) of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) in Raman spectra reveals an ordered carbon structure in it (Figure S14). \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) retains the octahedral shape of MET while presents hierarchical pore structure (Figure 1c,d; Figures S15 and S16). It possesses specific surface area of \(499\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) and mesopore proportion of 0.92 (pore-volume ratio). The pyrolyzed MET(Zn)- dcIm and \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\) also show similar morphology and rich mesopores (Figures S16 and S17, Table S3). The accessibility of these pores was further verified by assessing the electrochemical surface area (ECSA) of these samples (Figure S18). Abundant mesopores are important to the active site accessibility and the mass transfer during OPR.[10a] Additionally, EDS mapping verified the uniform dispersion of Fe, N, and Cl in the carbon matrix (Figure 1e,f) and uniformly dispersed Fe single atoms were clearly identified by aberration corrected high-angle annular darkfield scanning transmission electron microscopy (AC HAADF-STEM) (Figure 1g). Similarly, \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\) also presents uniformly distributed single Fe atoms (Figure S19).
To investigate the chemical state of N, C, and Cl in \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) , XPS was performed. \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) and the control samples exhibit similar C 1s XPS spectra (Figure S20). The samples display a N content of about \(10\,\mathrm{at}\,\%\) owing to the high N content in MET (Table S4). Notably, differing from pyrolyzed MET(Zn)- dcIm, samples of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) and \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\)
exhibit the Fe-N at \(399.0\,\mathrm{eV},\) which is attributed to \(\mathrm{FeN}_{x}\) species (Figure S21).[11] The Cl 2p spectrum also confirmed the existence of Cl in \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) (Figure S22). Further, the content of Fe and Cl in \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) was estimated as \(2.78\,\mathrm{wt}\,\%\) and \(0.32\,\mathrm{at}\,\%\) , according to the ICP-OES and XPS results, respectively.
Synchrotron-radiation-based X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) measurements were conducted to confirm the local structure of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) . According to the Fourier transforms (FT) of EXAFS spectra (Figure 2a), the peak at \(\approx\!1.5\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) is associated with the scattering of Fe-N coordination and the peak at \(\approx\!1.71\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}\) corresponds to Fe-Cl coordination. No obvious Fe-Fe peak \((\approx\!1.2\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}})\) were detected. Besides, the wavelet transform (WT) contour plot (Figure 2c) of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) exhibited slightly higher intensity maximum \((4.1\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\) than \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\) \((3.4\,\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{-1})\) , which is attributed to the Fe-Cl bonding. The above results indicate that Fe atoms are atomically dispersed and coordinated by N and Cl atoms. The EXAFS fitting shows that the first shell of the Fe atom in \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) exhibited an average coordination number of 3.5 for Fe-N and 1.2 for Fe-Cl (Figure 2b; Table S5). The d orbitals of the Fe atoms perpendicular to the Fe-N-C plane is not fully occupied and can potentially interact with Cl atoms.[3a] Mössbauer spectrum also indicates the bonding of Cl with the Fe sites (Figure S23, Table S6). Therefore, we inferred that \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) possesses a \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}\) configuration,
that is, \(\mathrm{Fe-N}_{4}\) configuration in the plane and Fe-Cl coordination in the axial. As a comparison, the Fe atoms in \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\) exhibited the \(\mathrm{Fe-N}_{4}\) coordination structure (Figure S24). As shown in Fe K-edge X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra (Figure 2d), the Fe species in \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) are positive charged with the valence state between 2 and 3. Additionally, the valence state of Fe species is lightly reduced in \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) than \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\) (Figure 2e), which is also verified by the XPS Fe 2p spectra (Figure S25, Table S7) and could be attributed to the Cl coordinated in the axial. The addition of axial Cl atoms could adjust the charge state of single Fe centers, resulting in an favorable binding energy of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\).[12]
The ORR performance of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) and the control samples was evaluated in a three-electrode system with calibrated reference electrodes (Figure S26). Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) and cyclic voltammograms (CVs, Figure S27) in \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\)-saturated \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH shows that \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) exhibited the optimal ORR activity among commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\), \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\), and pyrolyzed MET(Zn)-dcIm with a positive halfwave \((E_{1/2})\) potential of \(0.91\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE and a kinetic current density \((J_{\mathrm{k}})\) of \(107\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) at \(0.85\,\mathrm{V}\) vs. RHE (Figure 3a,b; Table S9). Besides, the ORR activity of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) also outperforms most of the reported MOF-derived SACs (Table S8). Pyrolyzed MET(Zn)-dcIm exhibited moderate activity which can be ascribed to the hierarchically porous N-doped carbon matrix. The ORR kinetics of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) is further confirmed by a smaller Tafel slope of \(35.8\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1}\) than that of \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}/\mathrm{NC}\) \((49.3\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1})\) and \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) \((66.5\,\mathrm{mV\,dec}^{-1}\), Figure 3c). RRDE measurements indicate that \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) possesses an average electron transfer number \((n)\) of 3.97 and the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) yield is below \(2\,\%\), indicating the highly efficient four-electron process (Figure 3d), which is further confirmed by Koutecky–Levich (K–L) plots (Figure S28). To investigate the effect of different Fe contents on
ORR performance, analogues with different Fe contents were prepared. All the samples show similar morphologies but the Fe sites exist in different forms (Figures S29 and S30). \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) exhibited the largest \(E_{1/2}\) and \(J_{\mathrm{k}}\) among these samples, indicating the optimal Fe content (Figure 3e; Figure S31).
\(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) also presents high long-term stability. Only a slight negative shift in \(E_{1/2}\) was observed after 5000 continuously potential cycles (Figure 3 f). Fe single atoms were well preserved in \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) after cycling, according to the HAADF-STEM images (Figure S32), ICP, and XPS results (Figure S33). Besides, \(\mathrm{FeN}_{4}\mathrm{Cl}_{1}/\mathrm{NC}\) exhibited methanol tolerance (Figure S34), showing the
To further evaluate the practical application, as proof-of-concept, \(\mathrm{FeN_{4}C l_{1}/N C}\) was employed as the cathode catalyst in Zn-air battery (Figure 4a). The \(\mathrm{FeN_{4}C l_{1}/N C}\) -based battery exhibits a stable and high open circuit voltage (OCV) of \(1.47\,\mathrm{V}\) (Figure 4b), maximum power density of \(170\,\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 4c), specific capacity of \(801\,\mathrm{mAh\,g_{zn}}^{-1}\) at \(10\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) , and energy density of \(1055\,\mathrm{Wh\,kg_{zn}}^{-1}\) , which are higher than that of the commercial \(\mathrm{Pt}/\mathrm{C}\) -based battery \((1.38\,\mathrm{V},\) \(120\,\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , \(780\,\mathrm{mAh\,g_{zn}}^{-1}\) , and \(1015\,\mathrm{Wh\,kg_{zn}}^{-1}\) , Figure 4d), indicating the superior activity of \(\mathrm{FeN_{4}C l_{1}/N C}\) . Moreover, the \(\mathrm{FeN_{4}C l_{1}/N C}\) -based Zn-air battery shows excellent long-term stability for discharging over \(100\,\mathrm{h}\) with negligible voltage loss at \(10\,\mathrm{mA\,cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 4e), which is compared favorably to most reported SACs (Table S10). An electric fan was powered by one \(\mathrm{FeN_{4}C l_{1}/N C}\) -based Zn-air battery (Figure 4 f), demonstrating the feasible application of \(\mathrm{FeN_{4}C l_{1}/N C}\) in practical devices.
In summary, a \(Z\mathrm{n/Fe}\) -bimetallic MET modified with 4,5-dichloroimidazole is synthesized as a precursor to fabricate Fe SACs. Benefiting from a rich concentration of nitrogen atoms in triazole and uniform doping of heteroatoms, as well as the unique nature of MET, Fe single atoms
with high density and a \(\mathrm{FeN_{4}C l_{1}}\) configuration are obtained in a hierarchically porous nitrogen-doped carbon matrix. The catalyst exhibits outstanding ORR activity with a half-wave potential \((E_{1/2})\) of \(0.91\,\mathrm{V}\) and \(0.79\,\mathrm{V}\) in \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) KOH and \(0.1\,\mathrm{M}\) \(\mathrm{HClO_{4}}\) , respectively. This work provides a new perspective for the selection and design of MOFs as SAC precursors to achieve a high metal atom loading density and regulation of active sites, as well as enhancement of mass transfer at the same time, which may be extended to the design and optimization of other catalysts with high efficiency.
